(Regression test for bug #44: the buffer sinks - `set`, `copy` and
 `append` - shared Print's name resolver but not its VALUE renderer, so a
 `{list}` or `{map}` part reached the integer formatter and the buffer
 received the collection's heap address in decimal.

 LANGUAGE.md "Format Strings Everywhere" names these three sinks and says
 all sinks render identically; the Float arm of the same shared renderer
 was added for bug #1 for exactly this reason.

 Acceptance: every buffer line matches the Print control above it, and the
 collection composes with surrounding literal text and with a second
 append.)

a list called flat is [1, 2, 3].
a map called person is {"name": "Ada", "born": 1815}.

Create a buffer called out.

Print "copy: {flat}".
copy "copy: {flat}" to out.
Print out.

Print "set: {person}".
set out to "set: {person}".
Print out.

Print "set: {person} and {flat}".
append " and {flat}" to out.
Print out.

(A fixed-size buffer takes the rendered text, not an address, too.)
a buffer called small is 64 bytes in size.
copy "fixed: {flat}" to small.
Print small.
